me think about the FTS5 table structure and the snippet function:\n\n```sql\nCREATE VIRTUAL TABLE notes_fts USING fts5(title, body, content=notes, content_rowid=id);\n```\n\nThis creates an FTS5 table that uses the `notes` table as content. With `content=notes`, the FTS table doesn't store a separate copy of the data - it just stores the index. But this means we need to use `content_sync=` or triggers to keep it in sync.\n\nActually, with a content table (external content), FTS5 does NOT automatically sync. We need triggers or manual sync. The content table approach is more storage-efficient but requires careful sync.\n\nAlternatively, I can create a standalone FTS5 table (without `content=`) and use triggers to keep it in sync. This is simpler and more reliable.\n\nLet me go with the standalone approach with triggers:\n\n```sql\nCREATE VIRTUAL TABLE IF NOT EXISTS notes_fts USING fts5(title, body);\n\n-- Trigger on INSERT\nCREATE TRIGGER IF NOT EXISTS notes_fts_insert AFTER INSERT ON notes BEGIN\n  INSERT INTO notes_fts(rowid, title, body) VALUES (new.id, new.title, new.body);\nEND;\n\n-- Trigger on UPDATE\nCREATE TRIGGER IF NOT EXISTS notes_fts_update AFTER UPDATE ON notes BEGIN\n  INSERT INTO notes_fts(notes_fts, rowid, title, body) VALUES ('delete', old.id, old.title, old.body);\n  INSERT INTO notes_fts(rowid, title, body) VALUES (new.id, new.title, new.body);\nEND;\n\n-- Trigger on DELETE\nCREATE TRIGGER IF NOT EXISTS notes_fts_delete AFTER DELETE ON notes BEGIN\n  INSERT INTO notes_fts(notes_fts, rowid, title, body) VALUES ('delete', old.id, old.title, old.body);\nEND;\n```\n\nWait, for the UPDATE trigger, I need to delete the old entry and insert the new one. With a standalone FTS5 table, the way to delete is:\n```sql\nINSERT INTO notes_fts(notes_fts, rowid, title, body) VALUES ('delete', old.id, old.title, old.body);\n```\n\nThis is the correct way to delete from a standalone FTS5 table.\n\nNow for the search query:\n```sql\nSELECT \n  notes.id,\n  notes.title,\n  snippet(notes_fts, 0, '<mark>', '</mark>', '...', 20) as snippet,\n  rank\nFROM notes_fts\nJOIN notes ON notes.id = notes_fts.rowid\nWHERE notes_fts MATCH ?\nORDER BY rank\nLIMIT ? OFFSET ?\n```\n\nWait, `rank` in FTS5 is by default the `bm25()` value. When I use `ORDER BY rank`, it orders by bm25 where lower values are more relevant. But the test checks that ranks are in ascending order (lower = more relevant), so `ORDER BY rank` should work.\n\nActually, let me check the FTS5 rank column. By default, FTS5 uses `bm25()` as the rank, and lower values indicate better matches. So `ORDER BY rank` (ascending) puts the most relevant first. The test expects `results[i].rank >= results[i-1].rank`, which means ascending order. Good.\n\nFor the snippet function: `snippet(fts_table, column_index, start_marker, end_marker, ellipsis, num_tokens)`\n- column_index: 0 for title, 1 for body\n- I want to search both title and body, but show snippets from the body (or from whichever column has the match)\n\nActually, looking at the test more carefully, the snippet should show context around the matched term. Let me use the snippet function on both columns and pick the one that has the match. Or I could use a combined approach.\n\nActually, FTS5's `snippet()` function works on a per-column basis. If I search both title and body, I can get snippets from either. But the typical approach is to get the snippet from the body column (index 1), since that's where the main content is.\n\nBut wait, what if the match is in the title but not the body? Then the body snippet would be empty or not show the match. Let me think...\n\nActually, looking at the FTS5 `highlight()` and `snippet()` functions: they return the text of the column with markup around matched terms. If a column doesn't have any matches, `snippet()` would still return a portion of the text (just without any `<mark>` tags).\n\nFor the test \"Snippets contain `<mark>` tags around matched terms\", the search is for \"python\".